   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.14",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = (x² − x − 2)/(x² − 4). Which statement correctly describes the behavior of f near the zeros of its denominator and identifies every vertical asymptote of the graph of f?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→−2⁻} f(x) = ∞ and lim_{x→−2⁺} f(x) = −∞, so x = −2 and x = 2 are vertical asymptotes.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats every zero of the denominator as a vertical asymptote without checking for a common factor. At x = 2 the factor (x − 2) cancels, so f approaches 3/4, a finite value, and the graph has a hole there rather than unbounded behavior. Fix: Factor and cancel common factors first, then compute the limit at each remaining denominator zero to confirm it is infinite."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→−2⁻} f(x) = −∞ and lim_{x→−2⁺} f(x) = ∞, so x = −2 is the only vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is a sign error, usually from assuming the numerator is positive near x = −2. The numerator x + 1 approaches −1, so a negative numerator over a negative denominator on the left gives +∞, and over a positive denominator on the right gives −∞. Fix: Plug a test value such as x = −2.1 or x = −1.9 into the simplified form to check each side's sign."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→−2⁻} f(x) = ∞ and lim_{x→−2⁺} f(x) = −∞, so x = −2 is the only vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Factoring gives f(x) = (x − 2)(x + 1)/((x − 2)(x + 2)) = (x + 1)/(x + 2) for x ≠ 2, so x = 2 is a hole where f approaches 3/4. Near x = −2 the numerator approaches −1. The denominator is negative on the left, making f → ∞, and positive on the right, making f → −∞. One infinite one-sided limit is enough for an asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→−2} f(x) does not exist because the one-sided limits disagree, so the graph of f has no vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses a two-sided limit failing to exist with the absence of an asymptote. A vertical asymptote at x = −2 requires only that at least one one-sided limit be ∞ or −∞, and here both are, so stopping at does not exist loses the behavior description. Fix: When a limit fails to exist, ask whether either one-sided limit is infinite, and if so name x = c as the vertical asymptote."
     }
    ]
   },
