   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.14",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = (x² − 1)/(x² − 3x + 2). Which statement correctly describes the behavior of f near the values of x where its denominator equals zero?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→2⁻} f(x) = −∞ and lim_{x→2⁺} f(x) = ∞, so x = 2 is the only vertical asymptote of the graph of f.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Factoring gives f(x) = (x − 1)(x + 1)/((x − 1)(x − 2)) = (x + 1)/(x − 2) for x ≠ 1, so the limit at x = 1 is −2, which is finite. Near x = 2 the numerator approaches 3 while the denominator approaches 0, negative from the left and positive from the right. This unbounded behavior on each side makes x = 2 a vertical asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The graph of f has vertical asymptotes at x = 1 and x = 2.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap treats every zero of the denominator as an asymptote without simplifying first. The factor x − 1 cancels, and lim_{x→1} f(x) = 2/(−1) = −2, which is finite, so x = 1 is a hole rather than an asymptote. Fix: Factor and cancel common factors, then compute the limit at each zero of the denominator before naming an asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→2} f(x) = ∞, so x = 2 is the only vertical asymptote of the graph of f.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap comes from checking only the right side of x = 2, where f grows without bound in the positive direction. From the left, the denominator is negative, so f decreases without bound. The two-sided limit therefore is not ∞. Fix: Test a value slightly less than 2 and a value slightly greater than 2 to find the sign of f on each side."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim_{x→2} f(x) does not exist because the one-sided limits differ, so x = 2 is not a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap assumes that a nonexistent limit rules out an asymptote. In fact, a single one-sided limit of ±∞ is enough for x = 2 to be a vertical asymptote, and here both one-sided limits are infinite. Writing only that the limit does not exist hides that unbounded behavior. Fix: Ask whether either one-sided limit is ∞ or −∞, and if so, write that limit and name the asymptote."
     }
    ]
   },
