   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.14",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "A function f satisfies f(2) = 5. As x approaches 2 from the left, f(x) decreases without bound. As x approaches 2 from the right, f(x) increases without bound. Which statement correctly describes the one-sided limits and the vertical-asymptote behavior at x = 2?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim(x → 2⁻) f(x) = +∞ and lim(x → 2⁺) f(x) = +∞; x = 2 is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sign-of-infinity error: unbounded behavior is not always positive. The left-hand values decrease without bound, so that limit is −∞. Fix: Match each side’s description to its sign: increasing without bound means +∞, and decreasing without bound means −∞."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim(x → 2⁻) f(x) = −∞ and lim(x → 2⁺) f(x) = +∞; x = 2 is not a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Matching-sides misconception: opposite infinite limits prevent a two-sided limit, but they do not prevent a vertical asymptote. Unbounded behavior from even one side is sufficient. Fix: Check each one-sided limit for +∞ or −∞ before deciding whether the input identifies a vertical asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim(x → 2⁻) f(x) = −∞ and lim(x → 2⁺) f(x) = +∞; x = 2 is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Decreasing without bound gives the left-hand limit −∞, while increasing without bound gives the right-hand limit +∞. An infinite limit from either side establishes a vertical asymptote at x = 2, regardless of f(2)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "lim(x → 2⁻) f(x) = 5 and lim(x → 2⁺) f(x) = 5; x = 2 is not a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Point-value substitution error: f(2) describes the value at the input, not the behavior nearby. The stated unbounded behavior determines the one-sided limits and establishes a vertical asymptote despite f(2) = 5. Fix: Set aside f(2) and use the stated nearby behavior to determine each one-sided limit."
     }
    ]
   },
